  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, thanks for letting us know. I checked, and the second-floor printer (BL-PR-FL2) is offline on our network for everyone, so the problem is with the printer, not your computer. Could you or someone near it try these steps? 1) Look at the printer's screen for an error like a paper jam, no paper or toner, or an open door, and fix it if you see one. 2) Turn the printer off, wait 30 seconds, and turn it back on. 3) Make sure the network cable is plugged in firmly at the back and the little light next to it is on. Then reply and tell me what the screen showed and whether it's working again. I'll check it from our side once you do. If it's still offline after a restart, I'll send it to our network and hardware team. Until then, you can print to a printer on another floor. If it isn't on your list yet, add it under Settings > Bluetooth & devices > Printers & scanners > Add device. If it doesn't show up, choose 'Add manually' and type the name on the printer's label.",
  "internal_note": "User: James O'Connor (id 8), Operations. Device BL-DT-0108. I checked BL-PR-FL2 with check_device_status: online=false, no warnings, last check-in 2026-10-10T21:55:26Z. Following KB-022, the printer itself is at fault, not the user's machine. I asked the user to check the printer screen for errors, power cycle it, and check the network cable and link light. I pointed him to another printer in the meantime (KB-024). Next step: re-check device status after the user replies. Escalate to Tier 2 if it stays offline after a power cycle with no error shown, since that could be a network or hardware fault.",
